   787	  "_deny_baseline_comment": "PLAN-153 Wave E item 3 — coarse harness-level deny baseline (docs/deny-baseline.md). Entries 5-24 of permissions.deny below mirror scripts/install.sh DENY_BASELINE_ENTRIES exactly: credential-store reads (~/.ssh, ~/.aws, npm/gcloud/kube/docker/git-credentials/netrc/pypirc), dotenv-family reads (SPECIFIC .env variants only — a deny rule cannot carry allow exceptions, so .env.example/.sample/.template stay readable by omission; unlisted variants like .env.secret pass this backstop), and the curl|bash tripwire. Honest limits: Read deny does not cover arbitrary subprocesses opening files, Bash(curl * | bash) is bypassable by rephrasing (that class is owned by check_bash_safety.py), and **/.env is cwd-anchored. Gate floor: check_harness_config.py DENY_BASELINE (4 entries — PLAN-161 C1 removed the three Write() twins: on current CLIs (>=2.1.216) Edit(X) deny rules cover ALL file-editing tools and Write(X) rules are unconsulted; the Owner accepted the old-CLI residual) must remain a subset of this list.",
   788	  "_posture_comment": "PLAN-163 T5.3/OQ5(c) — Owner-ratified fail-closed session posture for the dogfood repo (CC 2.1.220 keys): permissions.defaultMode='manual' (2.1.2xx rename of the ask-first mode — every non-allowlisted tool call prompts). disableAutoMode was REMOVED 2026-08-03 (Owner decision, reversing the T5.3/OQ5(c) ratification): its observed effect is not only the documented 'no AUTOMATIC escalation mid-session' — it also strips 'auto' from the operator's native shift+tab mode cycle, i.e. the framework was removing a first-class Claude Code affordance from its own operator. Choosing the session permission mode is the operator's call, not the framework's. The fail-closed DEFAULT (defaultMode='manual') is unchanged: every session still STARTS ask-first; the operator can now cycle out of it deliberately, at the keyboard, for the session only. Measured 2026-08-03 with a neutralized user layer (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR): 'manual' prompts for Bash/Edit/Write; 'acceptEdits' does not. workflowSizeGuideline='medium' (advisory sizing guideline for Workflow-tool fan-outs; 2.1.220 binary schema: optional string enum ['unrestricted','small','medium','large'] — describe text: 'small' aims for fewer than 5 agents, 'medium' (the default) fewer than 15, 'large' fewer than 50, 'unrestricted' sends no guideline; a set value, including from managed settings, takes precedence over the /config toggle. Evidence: zod source at byte offset 226825131 of the 2.1.220 binary, sha256 8addc857..., T2.2 extraction. 'medium' matches the current session guideline, ~15 agents), sandbox.network.strictAllowlist=true + sandbox.filesystem.disabled=false above. Templates carry the same keys COMMENTED (adopters opt in). Rollback: to revert THIS change (the disableAutoMode removal), re-add the top-level key disableAutoMode with the value 'disable' — the string enum's sole legal value; a boolean makes 2.1.220 skip the WHOLE settings file (S286 live-fire). To roll back the remaining posture instead, remove defaultMode/workflowSizeGuideline + strictAllowlist in one commit — each key is independent and additive; no hook consumes them (harness-level only).",
   789	  "workflowSizeGuideline": "medium",
   790	  "_night_mode_deny_surface_comment": "PLAN-165 P1-corrected (D2 + round-2 findings NM-01/NM-03 + codex CX-1, Owner-ratified OQ1-redo 2026-08-03) — under defaultMode=acceptEdits a tool Edit/Write is auto-accepted, so every file that can DECIDE the next session's posture is deny-listed: (1) .claude/settings.local.json — the per-machine posture overlay (gitignored); a tool write of it is the direct escalation ladder. (2) .claude/state/night-mode.json — the night-mode marker/snapshot: night-mode off restores marker.prev_value into the overlay, so a marker write is a posture write BY PROXY (NM-01). (3) .claude/scripts/night-mode.py — the single sanctioned writer (NM-03). HONEST SCOPE (CX-1): these per-TOOL deny entries close only the Edit/Write rail — deny cannot see Bash. The load-bearing rail for Bash is _CANONICAL_GUARDS in check_canonical_edit.py (all three paths added there in the SAME ceremony; check_bash_safety keys off that list), plus kernel-tier listing of the two STATE files in check_arbitration_kernel.py. night-mode.py writes the overlay and the marker as a PROCESS (tempfile + os.replace), never via tools, so none of this costs a legitimate path — and per the Owner-ratified OQ1-redo, model-rail invocation of the toggle is itself blocked: on/off are HUMAN actions (terminal or the `!` prefix). The Write() twins were REMOVED in S292: the current harness (>=2.1.216) does NOT consult Write(path) rules in its file-permission check (the CLI emits an active warning for such rules) and the Edit(path) entries cover ALL file-editing tools — the same class PLAN-161 C1 removed from the credential-read baseline; the old-CLI residual documented there is now accepted for the posture files too, with the real defense on the _CANONICAL_GUARDS/kernel rails above (CX-1). Appended AFTER the curl|bash tripwire so entries 5-24 keep mirroring install.sh DENY_BASELINE_ENTRIES by position (see _deny_baseline_comment); these three entries are NOT part of that mirror. The three entries are byte-identical in templates/settings/settings.base.json (dogfood parity).",
